WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.600
Every boat has GPS now.

2
00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:05.800
So why did I just make you do this by hand?

3
00:00:06.050 --> 00:00:12.400
Because you should be keeping a log anyway: your courses, your speeds, and the time of every change.

4
00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:15.350
Your chartplotter track isn’t a DR.

5
00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:17.575
It shows where you’ve been.

6
00:00:17.825 --> 00:00:21.100
It says nothing about where you’ll be if the screen dies.

7
00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:28.325
And if the GPS or the power does fail, your last fix plus a DR from your log is how you get home.

8
00:00:28.575 --> 00:00:30.600
There’s a daily use too.

9
00:00:30.850 --> 00:00:33.750
Compare your DR with the GPS fix.

10
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.375
What does the gap between them tell you?

11
00:00:37.375 --> 00:00:40.225
How the current is setting you, and how hard.

12
00:00:40.475 --> 00:00:44.250
So practice on calm days, when nothing depends on it.

13
00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:46.425
That’s lesson seven.

14
00:00:46.675 --> 00:00:50.825
Circle for a fix, half circle for a DR, square for an E P.

15
00:00:51.075 --> 00:00:53.775
Plot on the hour and at every change.

16
00:00:54.025 --> 00:00:57.250
Next time: fixes from a single landmark.
